  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 225 AM MDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nThe drying trend will become more noticeable Saturday as PWATs drop \ninto the 0.5-1\" range. Western NM will get a much needed break from \nthe rain while central and eastern areas receive scattered showers \nand storms with fast storm motions. Sunday still looks like it will \nbe the driest day of the week with only low rain chances in the \neastern plains. \n\nThe combination of a backdoor front and the return of a subtropical \ntap (from troughing over California) will replenish moisture over \nNew Mexico early next week. More widespread, soaking rainfall is \nexpected although the exact trajectory of the sub-tropical tap will \ndetermine which areas are favored. This monsoon burst will be \nslightly different than the one from this week because an amplified \nsub-tropical jet stream will be playing a stronger role. Stronger \nforcing aloft means storms will be less dependent on afternoon \ndestabilization and increased shear will support the development of \nstorms that can sustain themselves over a longer period of time. \nFurthermore, storm motions will be faster, which is good news for \nsensitive areas such as recent burn scars. Long-term guidance is in \ngood agreement that above normal moisture will remain in place over \nthe desert southwest through the end of the month, keeping storm \nchances around.",
